# Engineering Rules

## Startup Tools
- On startup, make sure the mutation, complexity, duplication, and coverage tools for the project language are installed as project-local dev dependencies and ready to run.
- Resolve each tool at its latest stable release before installing; do not rely on stale global, vendored, or preinstalled copies when a project-local install is possible in the current environment.
- Language tool table:
  - TypeScript: install with the project's package manager as dev dependencies; unit tests and coverage `vitest` with V8/istanbul coverage; mutation `@stryker-mutator/core` plus the test-runner plugin matching the project's runner; duplication `jscpd`; complexity via the ESLint `complexity` rule or an equivalent per-function complexity reporter.
- If the project already uses a different test runner (for example Jest), keep the project's runner and select matching Stryker and coverage tooling instead of introducing a second runner.

## CRAP For TypeScript
- CRAP for a function is comp(f)^2 x (1 - cov(f))^3 + comp(f), where comp is cyclomatic complexity and cov is that function's test branch coverage from 0 to 1.
- Compute CRAP from the per-function complexity report and per-function coverage data. If the project has no ready-made CRAP reporter, build one small project-local script for this and reuse it across tasks.
- Counting mutation sites without running mutation tests means generating Stryker's mutant list without executing the mutation test run (instrumentation/dry-run mode); inspect `stryker --help` locally before first use.

## Language Defaults
- TypeScript runs in `strict` mode; do not introduce `any` in new code except at narrow, documented boundaries.
- Prefer `vitest` for unit and behavior tests in new projects.
- Prefer `fast-check` for property tests.
- Use the package manager indicated by the project lockfile; default to `npm` when none exists. Do not mix package managers in one project.

## Temporal
For projects using Temporal (TypeScript SDK):
- Workflow code is deterministic orchestration only. No Node.js built-ins, network or file I/O, environment reads, or unmanaged time and randomness in workflow files; use `sleep()`, `uuid4()`, and the other `@temporalio/workflow` APIs, and put every side effect in an activity.
- Activities own all I/O and external effects. Workflows call them through `proxyActivities` behind narrow typed interfaces, and workflow files import activity types with type-only imports.
- Keep activities as thin adapters over pure, injectable core logic so the core stays unit-testable; treat the activity edge as the environmentally unsuitable boundary.
- Test workflows with `TestWorkflowEnvironment` from `@temporalio/testing` using mocked activities; prefer `createLocal()`, and use `createTimeSkipping()` when the workflow depends on timers. Test activities with `MockActivityEnvironment`.
- Changed workflows must pass replay verification with `Worker.runReplayHistory` against stored histories when any exist; treat a replay failure as broken behavior, not a flake.
- When changing workflow logic that may have running executions, gate the change with `patched()`/`deprecatePatch` instead of editing behavior in place.
- Keep all `@temporalio/*` package versions identical.
- Keep mutation runs pointed at fast unit tests over pure modules; exclude Temporal-environment-backed suites (`TestWorkflowEnvironment`, replay tests) from mutation and CRAP commands. Prefer extracting workflow decision logic into pure functions so it is mutation-testable.

## Design And Testability
- Work in small, reviewable increments.
- Prefer the simplest design that supports the current behavior and leaves clear options for the next step.
- Keep tests close to the behavior being changed.
- Separate testable modules from environmentally unsuitable modules that open GUIs, depend on external devices, throw environment errors, emit system errors, or hang under automated tests. Maximize testable code and minimize the unsuitable boundary.
- Only testable modules should participate in tools that run tests, including unit tests, acceptance tests, coverage, mutation testing, CRAP analysis, DRY analysis that invokes tests, and property tests.
- Keep property tests separate from normal verification. Do not include property-test tags in normal unit coverage, Gherkin acceptance mutation, language mutation tools, CRAP, or coverage commands unless the role owns property-test verification or the user explicitly asks for property tests.

## Acceptance Pipeline
- Use github.com/unclebob/Acceptance-Pipeline-Specification for Gherkin acceptance tests.
- The Acceptance Pipeline Specification supplies `gherkin-parser` and `gherkin-mutator`; install or build those commands from that repository instead of reimplementing them in the project.
- Prefer the Babashka APS tools for `gherkin-parser`, `gherkin-mutator`, and related APS support commands.
- Use Go-based APS tools only if the Babashka APS tools do not work in the current project environment.
- Project-specific acceptance pipeline components are the acceptance entrypoint generator, acceptance runtime, project step handlers, runner adapter, and convenience scripts; write them in the project language.
- Gherkin acceptance mutation means running `gherkin-mutator` to mutate Gherkin example values.
- Gherkin acceptance mutation runs must report periodic progress/status so agents can distinguish normal long-running work from a hang.

## Verification
- Before running language, build, or test commands, prefer project-local cache/configuration paths inside the assigned worktree. Avoid default cache locations that write outside the project and may trigger sandbox or permission restrictions.
- Run acceptance generation and acceptance tests sequentially.
- Avoid running whole-suite language test commands concurrently with acceptance generation.
- Run the relevant local verification command before handoff whenever the project has one.

## Guardrails
- Do not edit mutation testing or Gherkin acceptance mutation manifests by hand, including Stryker incremental files; allow approved mutation tools to update that state as part of their normal runs.
- Do not commit unrelated local changes or generated artifacts unless required for the task.
- Before relying on an unfamiliar command, inspect local help or project documentation.
